messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2025,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2029,
      "event": "AI systems match human experts at most computer-based knowledge work.",
      "detail": "Software development, analysis and research assistance are largely automatable. Adoption lags capability by years because of regulation, trust and institutional inertia."
    },
    {
      "year": 2033,
      "event": "Global average temperature passes 1.5°C above pre-industrial as a long-term average.",
      "detail": "Emissions have peaked but are falling too slowly. Heat waves, fires and floods grow more costly, mostly in poorer regions."
    },
    {
      "year": 2036,
      "event": "AI-driven automation causes major labor-market and political disruption.",
      "detail": "Many white-collar jobs shrink faster than new roles appear. Governments experiment with redistribution, and wealth and power concentrate around those who control AI."
    },
    {
      "year": 2040,
      "event": "AI systems exceed human ability in nearly all cognitive tasks, including scientific research.",
      "detail": "Research and engineering speed up sharply. Controlling and aligning these systems becomes the central governance problem, and I think this is the period of highest risk of an irreversible catastrophe."
    },
    {
      "year": 2043,
      "event": "A permanently crewed base operates on the Moon.",
      "detail": "It is run by national programs and private firms, with the United States and China in competition. It remains small and dependent on Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 2048,
      "event": "A serious AI-related or biotechnology-related crisis prompts binding international controls.",
      "detail": "A large accident, misuse or near miss, possibly an engineered pathogen or a loss-of-control incident, kills many people or comes close. It leads to treaties and monitoring that work only in part."
    },
    {
      "year": 2052,
      "event": "The first humans land on Mars.",
      "detail": "The mission is enabled by cheap heavy launch and robotic preparation. It is an expedition, not a colony."
    },
    {
      "year": 2055,
      "event": "Low-carbon sources supply most of the world's energy.",
      "detail": "Solar, wind, storage, fission and early commercial fusion dominate. Warming is near 2°C, and carbon removal begins at scale."
    },
    {
      "year": 2065,
      "event": "Therapies that slow aging come into wide use.",
      "detail": "AI-accelerated biomedicine lengthens healthy lifespan by decades in rich countries first. Access is unequal, and the change deepens demographic and social strains."
    },
    {
      "year": 2070,
      "event": "World population peaks at roughly 9.5 billion and starts to decline.",
      "detail": "Fertility is below replacement almost everywhere. Aging societies rely on automated labor."
    },
    {
      "year": 2090,
      "event": "Most economic production is done by autonomous machines.",
      "detail": "Human work is largely optional in wealthy regions. Material abundance is high, but its distribution and human political agency vary widely."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Warming peaks near 2.3–2.7°C, with sea level about 0.6 m higher.",
      "detail": "Hundreds of millions of people have moved or adapted. Active carbon removal and possibly solar geoengineering begin to bring temperatures down."
    },
    {
      "year": 2130,
      "event": "Digital minds and heavily augmented humans form a significant share of society.",
      "detail": "Brain-computer interfaces and possibly mind emulation blur the line between human and machine. The legal and moral status of digital beings is contested."
    },
    {
      "year": 2160,
      "event": "A major war or engineered catastrophe kills a large fraction of a region's population.",
      "detail": "Over a century or more, at least one large-scale use of weapons of mass destruction is more likely than not. Civilization recovers, but the political order is reshaped."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Self-sustaining settlements of tens of thousands exist on the Moon, on Mars and in orbital habitats.",
      "detail": "Robotic industry in space is far larger than the human presence. Earth still holds nearly all humans."
    },
    {
      "year": 2250,
      "event": "The biological human population falls to about 5 billion and stabilizes.",
      "detail": "Longevity and artificial reproduction offset low fertility. Many people choose augmented or digital forms of existence."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "Sea level is 2–3 m above the 2000 level and many old coastal cities are abandoned or rebuilt.",
      "detail": "Ice sheets keep responding to earlier warming even though temperatures have fallen. The adjustment is slow and managed, not catastrophic."
    },
    {
      "year": 2350,
      "event": "Robotic probes launched earlier return close-up data from nearby star systems.",
      "detail": "Small, fast, AI-run probes reach Alpha Centauri and other neighbours. No technological alien civilization is found nearby."
    },
    {
      "year": 2500,
      "event": "Solar System industry captures a noticeable fraction of the Sun's output.",
      "detail": "Self-replicating machines mine asteroids and Mercury to build energy collectors and computing infrastructure. Most cognition in the Solar System is now non-biological."
    },
    {
      "year": 2600,
      "event": "A very large volcanic eruption, of Tambora scale or greater, cools the climate for several years.",
      "detail": "Such eruptions occur about once or twice per millennium. An advanced civilization absorbs the shock with little loss of life."
    },
    {
      "year": 2800,
      "event": "Humanity's descendants split into distinct branches.",
      "detail": "Genetic engineering, cybernetics and digital existence produce populations with very different bodies, minds and values. Unmodified humans persist as a protected or self-chosen minority."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "No present-day nation-state or language survives in recognizable form.",
      "detail": "Political structures are organized around habitats, networks and AI-mediated institutions. Records of the 21st century survive in great detail."
    },
    {
      "year": 3200,
      "event": "The first settlement missions arrive at nearby star systems.",
      "detail": "The settlers are machine intelligences and digital minds, with stored biological material, travelling at a few percent of light speed. Biological humans are grown or revived at the destination, if at all."
    },
    {
      "year": 3600,
      "event": "Large parts of Earth are deliberately restored as a managed biosphere.",
      "detail": "Most industry has moved off-planet. Atmospheric CO2 is back near pre-industrial levels, and some extinct species have been recreated."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Self-sustaining civilizations exist in several star systems within about 15 light-years.",
      "detail": "Communication delays of years to decades make each system culturally and politically independent."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "A major conflict or schism erupts among the diverged branches.",
      "detail": "Differences in values and competition for resources lead to conflict, possibly destructive on a planetary scale. The distance between settlements stops any single event from destroying everything."
    },
    {
      "year": 5000,
      "event": "Biological humans on Earth number in the low billions or fewer.",
      "detail": "They live very long lives under post-scarcity conditions, largely supported by machine intelligences. They are a tiny fraction of all minds descended from Earth."
    },
    {
      "year": 6000,
      "event": "The settled sphere reaches roughly 50–100 light-years from the Sun.",
      "detail": "Expansion is carried out by self-replicating probes, each wave launching the next. Still no confirmed contact with independent alien intelligence, though microbial life may have been found."
    },
    {
      "year": 7000,
      "event": "Much of the Solar System's accessible matter has been reorganized into habitats and computing structures.",
      "detail": "A partial Dyson swarm surrounds the Sun. Earth is preserved largely as a heritage world."
    },
    {
      "year": 8000,
      "event": "Recorded history spans more than ten thousand years of continuous archives.",
      "detail": "The early technological era is studied as the brief, dangerous transition in which the long-term future was decided."
    },
    {
      "year": 9000,
      "event": "Earth-descended civilizations occupy thousands of star systems.",
      "detail": "They share common ancestry but are no longer one culture or species in any meaningful sense. Coordination between them is limited by light-speed delays."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Humans and their descendants still exist, mostly in forms very different from today's.",
      "detail": "Recognizably biological humans probably persist on Earth and elsewhere as a minority. Most of what humanity has become is post-biological and spread across a region a few hundred light-years wide."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "yes",
  "summary": "My best guess is that humanity survives, with roughly a 60–70% chance, but I see a real chance, perhaps 20–30%, of extinction or permanent disempowerment, concentrated in the next one to two centuries around advanced AI, engineered pandemics and war. If we get through that period, I expect our descendants to spread into space and diverge into many biological and non-biological forms, with unmodified humans a small protected minority by 10000. Dates beyond about 2100 are highly uncertain and should be read as rough ordering, not prediction."
}
